1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,115
Hello! Today we're at '한라산' [han-ra-san] (Hallasan).

2
00:00:03,115 --> 00:00:06,790
It's a mountain beautiful in all four seasons.

3
00:00:08,230 --> 00:00:10,548
'날씨' [nal-ssi] means the weather.

4
00:00:10,548 --> 00:00:13,990
Clear, cloudy, rain, snow — it changes every day.

5
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:23,657
When you meet someone, ask: '날씨가 어때요' [nal-ssi-ga-eo-ttae-yo]?

6
00:00:23,657 --> 00:00:25,410
(How's the weather?).

7
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:27,640
A great way to start a chat.

8
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:30,471
Korea has four seasons.

9
00:00:30,471 --> 00:00:33,270
We call them '사계절' [sa-gye-jeol].

10
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:42,757
'봄' [bom] (spring), '여름' [yeo-reum] (summer), '가을' [ga-eul] (fall),

11
00:00:42,757 --> 00:00:44,795
'겨울' [gyeo-ul] (winter).

12
00:00:44,795 --> 00:00:50,180
Hallasan changes clothes each season.

13
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:53,467
Shall we meet Hallasan's four seasons one by one?

14
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:55,840
The weather and scenery change each time.

15
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:05,355
'-에는' [-e-neun] marks a season: 봄에는 (in spring),

16
00:01:05,355 --> 00:01:07,740
겨울에는 (in winter).

17
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:11,219
Let's start with spring.

18
00:01:11,219 --> 00:01:13,400
Shall we go to Hallasan in spring?

19
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,133
It's '봄' [bom] — spring.

20
00:01:17,133 --> 00:01:20,740
The frozen ground melts and days grow warm.

21
00:01:23,390 --> 00:01:26,395
'따뜻하다' [tta-tteut-ha-da] — to be warm.

22
00:01:26,395 --> 00:01:29,320
Spring isn't cold or hot. Just right.

23
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:35,688
'봄에는 따뜻해요' [bom-e-neun-tta-tteut-hae-yo] (In spring, it's warm).

24
00:01:35,688 --> 00:01:39,610
Add '-아요/어요' [--a-yo-/-eo-yo] to adjectives.

25
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:43,069
'꽃이 피다' [kkoch-i-pi-da] — flowers bloom.

26
00:01:43,069 --> 00:01:45,100
In spring, flowers open up.

27
00:01:45,550 --> 00:01:48,888
This is '선작지왓' [seon-jak-ji-wat] (Seonjakjiwat). In spring,

28
00:01:48,888 --> 00:01:53,610
royal azaleas bloom like a pink sea — a garden in the sky!

29
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:59,243
'벚꽃' [beot-kkot] — cherry blossom.

30
00:01:59,243 --> 00:02:02,130
When wind blows, petals fly like snow.

31
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:07,412
'바람이 불다' [ba-ram-i-bul-da] — the wind blows.

32
00:02:07,412 --> 00:02:09,490
Spring wind is soft and warm.

33
00:02:09,940 --> 00:02:14,957
'봄바람이 불어요' [bom-ba-ram-i-bul-eo-yo] (Spring wind blows).

34
00:02:14,957 --> 00:02:18,680
Use '-이/가 불다' [--i-/-ga-bul-da] for wind.

35
00:02:19,130 --> 00:02:24,074
'맑다' [mak-da] — to be clear. No clouds, blue sky.

36
00:02:24,074 --> 00:02:27,000
'오늘은 맑아요' [o-neul-eun-mal-a-yo] (It's clear today).

37
00:02:27,450 --> 00:02:29,676
On a warm, clear spring day,

38
00:02:29,676 --> 00:02:32,458
we go on a '소풍' [so-pung] (picnic).

39
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:33,810
Jieun is excited!

40
00:02:34,260 --> 00:02:36,207
In spring, wear light clothes.

41
00:02:36,207 --> 00:02:37,570
A cardigan is enough.

42
00:02:39,610 --> 00:02:45,741
Spring: 따뜻하다 (warm), 꽃이 피다 (bloom),

43
00:02:45,741 --> 00:02:50,120
바람이 불다 (windy). Remember!

44
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:53,330
After spring? It gets hotter and hotter.

45
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:57,620
Now it's summer. Let me change clothes!

46
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:02,218
Now it's '여름' [yeo-reum] — summer.

47
00:03:02,218 --> 00:03:05,170
Hallasan is lush and green all over!

48
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:13,146
'덥다' [deop-da] — to be hot. Summer is very hot.

49
00:03:13,146 --> 00:03:15,480
'너무 더워요' [neo-mu-deo-wo-yo]! (So hot!)

50
00:03:15,930 --> 00:03:20,091
'여름에는 더워요' [yeo-reum-e-neun-deo-wo-yo] (In summer, it's hot).

51
00:03:20,091 --> 00:03:23,370
The opposite is 춥다 (cold).

52
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:26,579
'습하다' [seup-ha-da] — to be humid.

53
00:03:26,579 --> 00:03:29,840
Korean summer is hot and humid. Sticky!

54
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:35,876
Summer has '장마' [jang-ma] — the rainy season.

55
00:03:35,876 --> 00:03:39,730
It rains a lot. '비가 와요' [bi-ga-wa-yo] (It rains).

56
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:47,394
'-이/가 오다' [--i-/-ga-o-da] — 비가 오다 (rain), 눈이 오다 (snow).

57
00:03:47,394 --> 00:03:53,760
Use 오다 for what falls from the sky.

58
00:03:54,210 --> 00:03:55,523
When it rains,

59
00:03:55,523 --> 00:04:00,777
wear a '우비' [u-bi] (raincoat) and use a '우산' (umbrella).

60
00:04:00,777 --> 00:04:03,310
Jieun in a yellow raincoat!

61
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:04,549
In summer,

62
00:04:04,549 --> 00:04:09,760
a '소나기' [so-na-gi] (sudden shower) can pour down — hard and brief.

63
00:04:12,610 --> 00:04:13,654
After rain,

64
00:04:13,654 --> 00:04:18,021
'사라오름' [sa-ra-o-reum] (Saraoreum) forms a lake in its crater.

65
00:04:18,021 --> 00:04:19,160
So mystical!

66
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:22,099
Rain stops,

67
00:04:22,099 --> 00:04:26,284
step into shade — '시원하다' [si-won-ha-da] (cool).

68
00:04:26,284 --> 00:04:27,620
Ah, refreshing!

69
00:04:29,470 --> 00:04:33,423
On a hot day, head to '돈내코' [don-nae-ko] (Donnaeko) valley!

70
00:04:33,423 --> 00:04:35,400
Emerald water, so cool.

71
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:39,144
In hot summer,

72
00:04:39,144 --> 00:04:43,430
eat cool '수박' [su-bak] (watermelon). The best!

73
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:45,154
In summer,

74
00:04:45,154 --> 00:04:50,886
wear '반팔' [ban-pal] (short sleeves) and '반바지' [ban-ba-ji] (shorts).

75
00:04:50,886 --> 00:04:52,160
Stay cool!

76
00:04:52,610 --> 00:04:59,841
Summer: 덥다 (hot), 습하다 (humid), 비가 오다 (rains).

77
00:04:59,841 --> 00:05:04,180
Don't forget your umbrella!

78
00:05:04,630 --> 00:05:07,417
After the hot summer? Cool autumn comes.

79
00:05:07,417 --> 00:05:08,950
Let me change clothes!

80
00:05:11,630 --> 00:05:14,313
Now it's '가을' [ga-eul] — autumn.

81
00:05:14,313 --> 00:05:17,080
Hallasan is starting to turn red!

82
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:23,705
Autumn is '선선하다' [seon-seon-ha-da] (crisp).

83
00:05:23,705 --> 00:05:26,360
Not hot, not cold. Just right.

84
00:05:26,810 --> 00:05:30,058
'가을에는 시원해요' [ga-eul-e-neun-si-won-hae-yo] (In autumn, it's cool).

85
00:05:30,058 --> 00:05:32,470
A great season for a walk.

86
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:36,115
'하늘이 높다' [ha-neul-i-nop-da] — the sky is high.

87
00:05:36,115 --> 00:05:38,060
Autumn sky is high and blue.

88
00:05:38,510 --> 00:05:41,139
'단풍' [dan-pung] — autumn foliage.

89
00:05:41,139 --> 00:05:43,290
Leaves turn red and yellow.

90
00:05:45,200 --> 00:05:47,510
This is '영실기암' [yeong-sil-gi-am] (Yeongsil)!

91
00:05:47,510 --> 00:05:52,930
Foliage on these screen-like cliffs — Hallasan's finest view!

92
00:05:53,790 --> 00:05:56,447
'단풍이 들다' [dan-pung-i-deul-da] — leaves change color.

93
00:05:56,447 --> 00:05:59,790
Hallasan turns red, orange, and yellow!

94
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:08,450
Autumn '사라오름' [sa-ra-o-reum] reflects foliage in its crater lake — like a painting.

95
00:06:09,860 --> 00:06:12,601
Fallen leaves are '낙엽' [nak-yeop].

96
00:06:12,601 --> 00:06:15,020
Step on them — crunch, crunch!

97
00:06:18,370 --> 00:06:21,230
'건조하다' [geon-jo-ha-da] — to be dry.

98
00:06:21,230 --> 00:06:23,600
Autumn air is dry, not moist.

99
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:27,188
At '어리목' [eo-ri-mok] (Eorimok),

100
00:06:27,188 --> 00:06:31,606
silver '억새' [eok-sae] (grass) sways in the wind.

101
00:06:31,606 --> 00:06:32,710
Deep autumn.

102
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:38,189
Autumn has tasty food: '감' [gam] (persimmon),

103
00:06:38,189 --> 00:06:42,961
'밤' [bam] (chestnut), '고구마' [go-gu-ma] (sweet potato)!

104
00:06:42,961 --> 00:06:45,540
A full-belly season.

105
00:06:45,990 --> 00:06:46,804
In autumn,

106
00:06:46,804 --> 00:06:50,469
wear '긴팔' [gin-pal] (long sleeves) with a light jacket.

107
00:06:50,469 --> 00:06:52,830
Chilly mornings and evenings.

108
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,404
As autumn deepens? Now comes cold winter.

109
00:06:56,404 --> 00:06:57,700
Let me bundle up!

110
00:07:00,190 --> 00:07:03,081
Now it's '겨울' [gyeo-ul] — winter.

111
00:07:03,081 --> 00:07:06,410
White snow begins to fall on Hallasan!

112
00:07:08,780 --> 00:07:14,072
'춥다' [chup-da] — to be cold. Winter is very cold.

113
00:07:14,072 --> 00:07:16,340
'너무 추워요' [neo-mu-chu-wo-yo]! (So cold!)

114
00:07:16,790 --> 00:07:20,769
'겨울에는 추워요' [gyeo-ul-e-neun-chu-wo-yo] (In winter, it's cold).

115
00:07:20,769 --> 00:07:24,280
The opposite of spring's 따뜻하다.

116
00:07:24,730 --> 00:07:28,315
The season's first snow is '첫눈' [cheot-nun].

117
00:07:28,315 --> 00:07:30,270
Such an exciting moment!

118
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:37,066
'눈이 와요' [nun-i-wa-yo] (It's snowing).

119
00:07:37,066 --> 00:07:40,010
White snow falls thick and fast.

120
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:50,920
비가 오다 (rain), 눈이 오다 (snow) — both use '-이/가 오다' [--i-/-ga-o-da].

121
00:07:51,370 --> 00:07:52,846
When snow piles up,

122
00:07:52,846 --> 00:07:55,641
make a '눈사람' [nun-sa-ram] (snowman).

123
00:07:55,641 --> 00:07:57,350
Jieun is building one!

124
00:07:59,810 --> 00:08:04,407
Have a '눈싸움' [nun-ssa-um] (snowball fight) with friends.

125
00:08:04,407 --> 00:08:05,310
Winter fun!

126
00:08:07,290 --> 00:08:09,364
'얼다' [eol-da] — to freeze.

127
00:08:09,364 --> 00:08:11,757
When it's cold, water freezes.

128
00:08:11,757 --> 00:08:13,990
Rivers freeze, hands freeze!

129
00:08:16,190 --> 00:08:20,138
Below zero is '영하' [yeong-ha].

130
00:08:20,138 --> 00:08:24,350
'오늘은 영하 5도예요' [o-neul-eun-yeong-ha-5-do-ye-yo] (It's -5°C today).

131
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:28,679
At '1100고지' [1-1-0-0-go-ji],

132
00:08:28,679 --> 00:08:33,528
ice flowers bloom on branches — '상고대' [sang-go-dae].

133
00:08:33,528 --> 00:08:35,580
Sparkling like jewels!

134
00:08:38,330 --> 00:08:40,884
In winter, wear thick clothes — sweater, coat,

135
00:08:40,884 --> 00:08:41,550
and a scarf.

136
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:47,857
But Hallasan's peak is even colder.

137
00:08:47,857 --> 00:08:49,120
This isn't enough!

138
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:52,771
So I got fully ready!

139
00:08:52,771 --> 00:08:55,837
A thick long padded coat, fur hat, scarf,

140
00:08:55,837 --> 00:08:58,380
and gloves — '방한복' [bang-han-bok]!

141
00:09:01,630 --> 00:09:05,010
Now I'm not cold at all. Winter gear is the best!

142
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:10,719
Now let's go up to Baengnokdam at the very top!

143
00:09:10,719 --> 00:09:14,130
Let's start our '등산' [deung-san] (hike). Up we go!

144
00:09:17,170 --> 00:09:18,467
The peak at last!

145
00:09:18,467 --> 00:09:20,985
Snow-covered '백록담' [baek-rok-dam] (Baengnokdam).

146
00:09:20,985 --> 00:09:23,960
A white crater holding the whole world!

147
00:09:27,770 --> 00:09:30,439
Snowy Hallasan is truly magnificent.

148
00:09:30,439 --> 00:09:32,070
A white world unfolds!

149
00:09:33,760 --> 00:09:44,522
Winter: 춥다 (cold), 눈이 오다 (snows), 얼다 (freeze), 첫눈,

150
00:09:44,522 --> 00:09:47,750
상고대. Bundle up!

151
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:49,642
Spring, summer, autumn,

152
00:09:49,642 --> 00:09:51,900
winter — we circled all of Hallasan!

153
00:09:57,430 --> 00:10:06,783
따뜻하다 (warm), 덥다 (hot), 시원하다 (cool), 춥다 (cold).

154
00:10:06,783 --> 00:10:10,850
Each season differs.

155
00:10:11,300 --> 00:10:19,478
비가 와요 (rain), 눈이 와요 (snow), 바람이 불어요 (wind).

156
00:10:19,478 --> 00:10:21,190
Remember?

157
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:25,050
Which season do you like? Me… I like them all!

158
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:30,831
Hallasan shows a different face each season.

159
00:10:30,831 --> 00:10:32,410
What a wonderful mountain!

160
00:10:34,370 --> 00:10:36,650
See you next time. Goodbye!

